### Why does Kafka have no end-to-end backpressure?

Kafka deliberately decouples producers from consumers. A producer writes to the log and gets acknowledged based only on broker replication; it does not know or care how far behind consumers are. This decoupling is a feature for independent scaling, but it means the system will never automatically slow the producer to match a slow consumer.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class NoBackpressureExplained {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Kafka's design: no end-to-end backpressure");
        System.out.println("  Producer ack depends on: broker replication only");
        System.out.println("  Producer knows about consumer lag: NO");
        System.out.println("  System will slow producer for slow consumer: NO");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("If consumer rate < producer rate permanently:");
        System.out.println("  Lag grows without bound");
        System.out.println("  Latency climbs proportionally");
        System.out.println("  Eventually: retention deletes unread messages");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Kafka's design: no end-to-end backpressure
  Producer ack depends on: broker replication only
  Producer knows about consumer lag: NO
  System will slow producer for slow consumer: NO

If consumer rate < producer rate permanently:
  Lag grows without bound
  Latency climbs proportionally
  Eventually: retention deletes unread messages
```

### How do you measure consumer lag?

Lag is the difference between the log-end offset (newest record) and the consumer's committed offset. You read it with the consumer group describe command.

```bash
kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group settlement-service \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
```

```text
GROUP              TOPIC       PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG
settlement-service transfers   0          30000           45000           15000
settlement-service transfers   1          28000           44000           16000
settlement-service transfers   2          31000           46000           15000
# Lag growing every check -- structural imbalance
```

### What happens when lag exceeds retention?

When lag grows past the retention horizon, the broker deletes the oldest segments. The consumer's committed offset points at data that no longer exists, and it jumps forward based on `auto.offset.reset`, silently losing those messages.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class RetentionDataLoss {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long producerRate = 2000; // records/sec
        long consumerRate = 1200; // records/sec
        long deficit = producerRate - consumerRate; // 800/sec
        long retentionHours = 6;
        long retentionRecords = producerRate * retentionHours * 3600;
        long hoursToLoss = retentionRecords / deficit / 3600;

        System.out.println("Producer: " + producerRate + " rec/s");
        System.out.println("Consumer: " + consumerRate + " rec/s");
        System.out.println("Deficit:  " + deficit + " rec/s");
        System.out.println("Retention: " + retentionHours + " hours (" + retentionRecords + " records)");
        System.out.println("Time until data loss: ~" + hoursToLoss + " hours");
        System.out.println("  After this: oldest unread messages are deleted by retention");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Producer: 2000 rec/s
Consumer: 1200 rec/s
Deficit:  800 rec/s
Retention: 6 hours (43200000 records)
Time until data loss: ~15 hours
  After this: oldest unread messages are deleted by retention
```

### How do you fix the throughput imbalance?

The most direct fix is consumer parallelism: add consumers up to the partition count. Beyond that, add partitions to support more consumers, speed up each consumer, and batch expensive operations.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class ThroughputFixes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Fix 1: Scale consumer parallelism");
        System.out.println("  Add consumers to the group up to partition count");
        System.out.println("  If at limit, add partitions (keyed topic caveat)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Fix 2: Speed up each consumer");
        System.out.println("  Batch database writes (e.g., 100 inserts in one tx)");
        System.out.println("  Remove per-record synchronous calls to slow APIs");
        System.out.println("  Parallelize processing with a worker pool");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Fix 3: Tune consumer config");
        System.out.println("  Increase max.poll.records for bulk work");
        System.out.println("  Increase fetch.max.bytes for network efficiency");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Fix 1: Scale consumer parallelism
  Add consumers to the group up to partition count
  If at limit, add partitions (keyed topic caveat)

Fix 2: Speed up each consumer
  Batch database writes (e.g., 100 inserts in one tx)
  Remove per-record synchronous calls to slow APIs
  Parallelize processing with a worker pool

Fix 3: Tune consumer config
  Increase max.poll.records for bulk work
  Increase fetch.max.bytes for network efficiency
```

### How do you build application-level backpressure?

Since Kafka provides no backpressure, you build it yourself. Use `consumer.pause()` and `consumer.resume()` to stop fetching when the internal work queue is full.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;

public class AppBackpressure {
    private static final int QUEUE_CAPACITY = 1000;
    private static final BlockingQueue<String> workQueue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(QUEUE_CAPACITY);

    public static void managePressure(KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer) {
        Set<TopicPartition> assigned = consumer.assignment();

        if (workQueue.remainingCapacity() < 100) {
            consumer.pause(assigned);
            System.out.println("PAUSE: work queue near full (" + workQueue.size() + "/" + QUEUE_CAPACITY + ")");
        } else if (workQueue.size() < QUEUE_CAPACITY / 2) {
            consumer.resume(assigned);
            System.out.println("RESUME: work queue drained (" + workQueue.size() + "/" + QUEUE_CAPACITY + ")");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Application-level backpressure with pause/resume:");
        System.out.println("  Queue full -> pause(partitions) -> stop fetching");
        System.out.println("  Queue drained -> resume(partitions) -> fetch again");
        System.out.println("  poll() still called for heartbeats when paused");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Application-level backpressure with pause/resume:
  Queue full -> pause(partitions) -> stop fetching
  Queue drained -> resume(partitions) -> fetch again
  poll() still called for heartbeats when paused
```

### What about bursty vs structural imbalance?

If the imbalance is only bursty, lag is healthy and self-correcting. The log is the buffer designed to absorb bursts. Do not over-engineer for a steady-state problem you do not have.

| Scenario | Lag Behavior | Action |
|---|---|---|
| Bursty traffic | Spikes then drains | None -- log absorbs it |
| Structural imbalance | Grows indefinitely | Scale consumers, speed up processing |
| Single hot partition | One partition lagging | Key bucketing or salting |

### What metrics should you alert on?

Alert on lag trend, not just absolute lag. A steadily rising lag is the early warning of a structural imbalance long before retention deletes anything.

```bash
# Monitor lag trend over time
kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group settlement-service \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
# Run every 5 minutes; if LAG column increases each time -> structural problem
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Size retention so it exceeds your worst-case recovery time. Watch for a single hot partition masquerading as a global throughput problem. Reactive frameworks like Reactor Kafka expose real backpressure to the application layer by requesting only as many records as downstream demand allows.
